      "level": 1,
      "content": "Truthy values"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "All values other than the six falsy values are considered truthy in JavaScript. Here are some examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "items": [
        "true - The boolean value is true.",
        "Non-zero numbers - e.g., 1, -1, 3.14, etc.",
        "Non-empty strings - e.g., \"hello\", '0', \"false\", etc.",
        "Arrays - e.g., [], [1, 2, 3], etc..",
        "Objects - e.g., {}, { a: 1 }, etc..",
        "Functions - e.g., function() {}, () => {}, etc.."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1if([]){\n2    console.log(\"Truthy\");\n3}\n4else{\n5    console.log(\"Falsy\");\n6};"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Truthy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Since [] is a truthy, the if block statement is printed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
